WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.725
Gravitational lensing measures the Hubble constant
by comparing time delays between multiple

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:09.763
images of a distant source to a model of the lens
galaxy's mass.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:15.630
But lensing degeneracies allow many different mass
distributions to reproduce the same image,

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:19.542
so independent kinematic constraints are needed to
break them.

00:00:19.622 --> 00:00:20.867
The trouble is speed.

00:00:20.947 --> 00:00:24.905
Modeling galaxy kinematics with a physics code like
JAM is slow,

00:00:24.985 --> 00:00:31.719
and it must be recomputed for every single
likelihood evaluation inside a Markov Chain Monte Carlo sampling.

00:00:31.799 --> 00:00:38.344
This makes jointly exploring the full lensing and
kinematics parameter space computationally prohibitive,

00:00:38.424 --> 00:00:42.950
forcing modelers to cut corners by fitting the two
components separately.
